---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Core Assumption — Full Examination" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Considering Treatments Separately or Together" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Is It Probable the Treatment Will Be Accepted?" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Measurement — Most Likely Amount vs Expected Value" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Most Likely Amount Method" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Reassessment of Judgments and Estimates" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRIC 23 vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why uncertain tax treatments needed specific guidance</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 12 requires entities to recognise CURRENT TAX and DEFERRED TAX based on the tax treatment expected to be applied in the relevant jurisdiction. But when there is UNCERTAINTY over whether a particular tax treatment will be accepted by the tax authority, entities faced a dilemma: apply the treatment and risk reversal, or apply a more conservative treatment and overstate tax expense. IFRIC 23 resolves this by requiring entities to REFLECT THE UNCERTAINTY using either the most-likely-amount method or the expected-value method — a measurement approach rather than a policy choice.</div>
        <div class="note">IFRIC 23 deliberately does NOT apply IAS 37's provisions framework to uncertain tax treatments. Instead, it creates a MEASUREMENT approach within IAS 12's existing current/deferred tax framework — a critically important distinction that affects how changes in estimates are treated.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The Conceptual Framework at work</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 23 applies the Conceptual Framework's faithful representation principle to a measurement problem. The entity must recognise the UNCERTAIN TAX POSITION using the method that best ESTIMATES the amount that will be accepted by the tax authority. This is not a "provision for uncertain taxes" — it is a MEASUREMENT ADJUSTMENT to the current or deferred tax asset/liability itself, ensuring that the tax balance sheet reflects the best estimate of the tax authority's likely position.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRIC 23 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 23 applies to INCOME TAX TREATMENTS that are UNCERTAIN — specifically, where there is uncertainty over whether the relevant tax authority will accept a particular tax treatment under the applicable tax law. The interpretation applies to both CURRENT TAX (affecting taxable profit) and DEFERRED TAX (affecting temporary differences). IFRIC 23 does NOT apply to: (a) taxes other than income tax (e.g. VAT, sales tax, property tax), (b) tax treatments that are certain, or (c) tax treatments where the uncertainty is about the TIMING of recognition, not the AMOUNT.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL FACT PATTERNS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Transfer pricing arrangements where the tax authority may challenge the arm's-length nature of intercompany pricing</span>
          <span>Research and development tax credits where eligibility criteria are subject to interpretation</span>
          <span>Tax loss carryforwards where the authority may challenge the entity's ability to utilise them</span>
          <span>Permanent vs temporary difference classification where the tax treatment of a transaction is disputed</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Uncertain tax treatment</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A tax treatment (under applicable tax law) for which there is uncertainty over whether the relevant tax authority will accept it — the subject matter of IFRIC 23.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Most likely amount</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The amount that is most likely to be accepted by the tax authority — the IFRIC 23 measurement method used when there is a single most likely outcome.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Core Assumption — Full Examination</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The starting point for all uncertain tax analysis</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 23 requires the entity to ASSUME that THE TAX AUTHORITY WILL EXAMINE the tax treatment and has access to ALL RELEVANT INFORMATION. This means the entity cannot assume that the authority will NOT challenge the treatment, or that it will accept the treatment without examination. The entity must measure the uncertain tax position on the basis that the authority WILL review it and WILL have all relevant information available.</div>
        <div class="elabel">THE FULL EXAMINATION ASSUMPTION</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>The tax authority will examine the tax treatment</span>
          <span>The tax authority has access to all relevant information</span>
          <span>The measurement is based on the LIKELIHOOD of acceptance, not the DESIRABILITY of acceptance</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">⚠️ The full examination assumption means that entities cannot simply apply a tax treatment and hope the authority does not notice. IFRIC 23 forces entities to MEASURE the uncertainty as if it WILL be examined — a more conservative but more faithful approach.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Considering Treatments Separately or Together</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The separate vs combined analysis</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 23 requires the entity to consider EACH UNCERTAIN TAX TREATMENT SEPARATELY — unless the tax law requires them to be considered TOGETHER (e.g. where treatments are interdependent). Where treatments can be considered separately, each treatment is measured independently. Where they must be considered together, the combined effect is measured as a single uncertain tax position.</div>
        <div class="elabel">WHEN TO CONSIDER TREATMENTS TOGETHER</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>When the tax law treats the items as a single unit (e.g. a single transaction with multiple tax effects)</span>
          <span>When the treatments are INTERDEPENDENT — the acceptance of one depends on the acceptance of another</span>
          <span>When it is PRAGMATIC to consider them together because they are evaluated as a package by the tax authority</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The practical impact of separate consideration</div>
        <div class="principle">When treatments are considered separately, some treatments may be measured at 100% acceptance (no uncertainty), while others are measured using the most-likely or expected-value method. This granular approach means that only the genuinely uncertain positions carry the measurement burden — not the entire tax position.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Is It Probable the Treatment Will Be Accepted?</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">The probability threshold for recognition</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 23 requires the entity to assess whether it is PROBABLE that the tax treatment will be ACCEPTED by the tax authority. If it IS probable, the full amount of the tax asset/liability is recognised at the full tax treatment amount. If it is NOT probable, the entity must reduce the recognised amount using the most-likely or expected-value method — but the tax asset or liability is STILL recognised; it is not derecognised or deferred.</div>
        <div class="elabel">THREE RECOGNITION SCENARIOS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Treatment almost certain (e.g. 95%+ probability of acceptance): recognise the full amount</span>
          <span>Treatment uncertain but probable (e.g. 50-95% probability): recognise the full amount, but measure using most-likely/expected-value method</span>
          <span>Treatment unlikely to be accepted (e.g. <50% probability): measure using most-likely/expected-value method; the asset/liability may be significantly reduced</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info">Unlike IAS 37, which derecognises a provision when an outflow becomes remote, IFRIC 23 does NOT derecognise the tax asset or liability when acceptance is unlikely. The tax balance is ALWAYS recognised — only the MEASUREMENT changes based on the probability of acceptance.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Measurement — Most Likely Amount vs Expected Value</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The most-likely-amount method</div>
        <div class="principle">When there is a SINGLE OUTCOME that is MORE LIKELY than any other outcome — for example, the tax authority will accept 70% of a deduction but reject 30% — the entity uses the MOST-LIKELY AMOUNT method. This method selects the single most probable outcome as the measurement basis, without considering the full range of possible outcomes.</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-label">MOST-LIKELY AMOUNT</div>
          <div class="f-eq">Tax asset/liability = The single outcome most likely to be accepted by the tax authority</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The expected-value method</div>
        <div class="principle">When there are MULTIPLE POSSIBLE OUTCOMES with different probabilities — for example, the tax authority might accept 60%, 80%, or 100% of a deduction, each with a different probability — the entity uses the EXPECTED-VALUE method. This method calculates the WEIGHTED AVERAGE of all possible outcomes, weighted by their respective probabilities.</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-label">EXPECTED VALUE</div>
          <div class="f-eq">Tax asset/liability = SUM of (Outcome amount × Probability of that outcome) for all possible outcomes</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Most Likely Amount Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: R&D tax credit uncertainty</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A technology company claims £2m of R&D tax credits. Based on similar cases, the company assesses: 70% chance the authority accepts £1.5m, 20% chance it accepts £1.0m, and 10% chance it accepts £0.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer"><strong>Step 1 — Identify uncertain treatment:</strong> £2m R&D tax credit claim<br><br><strong>Step 2 — Apply most-likely-amount method:</strong><br>Most likely outcome = £1.5m (70% probability — the single most likely outcome)<br><br><strong>Step 3 — Recognise the tax asset:</strong><br>Tax credit receivable = £1.5m<br><br><strong>Step 4 — Expected value (for comparison):</strong><br>Expected value = (£1.5m × 70%) + (£1.0m × 20%) + (£0 × 10%) = £1.25m<br><br><strong>Step 5 — Method selection:</strong><br>Since £1.5m is the SINGLE most likely outcome (70% > any other probability), the most-likely-amount method is appropriate — recognising £1.5m rather than the expected value of £1.25m.<br><br><strong>Balance sheet impact:</strong><br>Tax credit receivable: £1.5m<br>Deferred tax asset (if timing difference): adjusted accordingly</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Reassessment of Judgments and Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">When the measurement must be updated</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 23 requires that the entity REEVALUATES its uncertain tax position at EACH REPORTING DATE, taking into account: (a) new information about the tax authority's likely position, (b) changes in tax law or enforcement practice, (c) the outcome of any ongoing examination or audit, and (d) changes in the entity's own facts and circumstances. Changes in the measurement of the uncertain tax position are treated as CHANGES IN ACCOUNTING ESTIMATES under IAS 8 — recognised prospectively in profit or loss.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TRIGGERS FOR REASSESSMENT</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>New tax authority guidance or rulings that affect the probability of acceptance</span>
          <span>Progress in an ongoing tax audit or examination</span>
          <span>Changes in tax legislation or case law</span>
          <span>New information about the entity's own facts (e.g. successful appeal in a similar case)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info">Because changes are treated as changes in accounting estimates (not changes in accounting policies), there is no restatement of prior periods — the adjustment is recognised prospectively in the period of change.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What IFRIC 23 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Description of the uncertain tax treatments and the nature of the uncertainty</span>
          <span>The amount of the current and deferred tax assets and liabilities affected by the uncertainty</span>
          <span>The measurement method used (most-likely amount or expected value) and the reasons for selecting it</span>
          <span>The assumptions and estimates underlying the measurement</span>
          <span>Changes in the measurement during the period and the reasons for those changes</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether a tax treatment is uncertain and therefore within IFRIC 23's scope</span>
              <span>Whether uncertain treatments should be considered separately or together</span>
              <span>Whether the most-likely-amount or expected-value method is more appropriate</span>
              <span>Whether it is probable the treatment will be accepted by the tax authority</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>The probability distribution of possible outcomes for each uncertain tax treatment</span>
              <span>The most likely outcome amount when using the most-likely-amount method</span>
              <span>The expected value of outcomes when using the expected-value method</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRIC 23 vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IFRIC 23 vs ASC 740</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRIC 23 (IFRS)</span><span>US GAAP (ASC 740)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Framework</span><span>Measurement adjustment within IAS 12 — NOT a provision under IAS 37</span><span>Morelihood standard — recognition threshold is "more likely than not" (>50%)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Recognition threshold</span><span>No derecognition — tax asset/liability always recognised; measurement adjusted</span><span>Derecognition when the tax position is NOT more likely than not to be sustained</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Measurement method</span><span>Most-likely amount or expected value</span><span>Cumulative probability method — the largest benefit with >50% probability of recognition</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Interest and penalties</span><span>Not addressed by IFRIC 23 — may be accounted for as tax expense or finance cost</span><span>Accrued as part of income tax expense under ASC 740</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Changes in estimates</span><span>Prospective recognition under IAS 8 (change in estimate)</span><span>Recognised in the period the determination is made</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRIC 23 event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Current tax asset/liability recognised with uncertainty</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Measured at most-likely or expected-value amount; difference vs tax return amount in profit or loss</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Deferred tax asset with uncertainty</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Measured at most-likely or expected-value; reversal of deferred tax in profit or loss</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Reassessment of uncertain tax position</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Change in estimate under IAS 8 — prospective recognition</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Interest and penalties on underpaid tax</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Typically recognised as tax expense or finance cost, not part of the IFRIC 23 measurement</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 12</span><span>The parent standard IFRIC 23 interprets, supplying the overall current and deferred tax accounting framework</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 37</span><span>Deliberately not applied to uncertain tax treatments — IAS 12's own measurement framework governs this area</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Governs whether a change in an uncertain tax position estimate is treated as a change in accounting estimate</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Uncertain tax treatment</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A tax treatment for which there is uncertainty over whether the relevant tax authority will accept it — the subject matter of IFRIC 23.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Most-likely amount</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The IFRIC 23 measurement method selecting the single most probable outcome when there is one outcome more likely than any other.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Uncertain tax treatment", definition: "A tax treatment (under applicable tax law) for which there is uncertainty over whether the relevant tax authority will accept it — the subject matter of IFRIC 23." },
      { term: "Most-likely amount", definition: "The IFRIC 23 measurement method that selects the single most probable outcome when there is one outcome more likely than any other." },
      { term: "Expected value", definition: "The IFRIC 23 measurement method that calculates the weighted average of all possible outcomes, weighted by their respective probabilities." },
      { term: "Full examination assumption", definition: "The IFRIC 23 assumption that the tax authority will examine the tax treatment and has access to all relevant information — the starting point for measuring uncertain tax positions." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRIC23} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>




